  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria testable="true">
    <criterion testable="true">Each structural op (split / merge / insert / delete paragraph) round-trips correctly on the corpus, including paragraph-mark deletion represented via `w:pPr/w:rPr/w:del`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Structural ops are applied LAST in the pass; a text op and a structural op on the same paragraph both land correctly (text-op anchors unaffected by the structural change).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">NEGATIVE: a merge of a paragraph with a following table (or a section-break boundary) is handled or refused, NOT corrupted (the package still opens in Word).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The engine stays stateless / `byte[]`-in/out / no AI internals (Tier-1 NetArchTest green); DOM is mutated, `document.xml` is never string-edited.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Publish size reported delta vs ~49.63 MB, ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE; BFF build + NetArchTest green; `/conflict-check` run before the PR.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>The paragraph-mark-deletion (merge) edge cannot be represented as a valid `w:del` on `w:pPr/w:rPr` for a corpus case (e.g. merging across a section break, into a table cell, or over a paragraph carrying list/numbering that Word will re-derive). STOP and surface per root §6 / §6.5 rather than emitting a package Word may repair or corrupt.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <notes>
    No `&lt;justification&gt;` — this MODIFIES task 030's engine (no new surface). Structural ops are the capability the retired paragraph-diff synthesizer never had; sequencing-last is the discipline that keeps text and structural ops composable in a single pass.
  </notes>

